on"} [:p {} "The 3-position small-bore event consisted of the scores from the prone small-bore event, with the competitors shooting an additional 40 rounds kneeling and standing. Kneeling was shot first, and Finland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/42059"} "Vilhon Ylönen"] " broke the world record for that discipline with 394 to take a decisive lead going into the final phase. But he faltered and finished second to Norway’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/43511"} "Erling Kongshaug"] ". Kongshaug was consistent. He did not win any of the phases, but he equaled Ylönen with 1,164 points for the event and won the tie-breaker with more shots in the inner ring (53-49). The bronze medal was won by the winner of the English match, " [:a {:href "/athletes/44498"} "Boris Andreyev"] " (URS). Kongshaug had won the 1950 World Championship with the same score of 1,164 and Andreyev had also finished second in that event."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {} "Center Hits"] [:th {} "Standing Points"] [:th {} "Kneeling Points"] [:th {} "Prone Points"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/43511"} "Erling Kongshaug"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {} "1,164"] [:td {} "53"] [:td {} "380"] [:td {} "387"] [:td {} "397"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/42059"} "Vilho Ylönen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FIN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FIN.png"}] "FIN"]] [:td {} "1,164"] [:td {} "49"] [:td {} "373"] [:td {} "